window.receiveProjectSource({"path": ".docs/specs/ingest/DESIGN-restricted-shared-datasets.md", "lines": ["# Design \u2014 Restricted Distribution and Event Participation of Platform-Owned Datasets", "", "| | |", "|---|---|", "| **Status** | Draft \u2014 product decisions resolved; Opus 5.5 adversarial review approved; implementation/Jira mapping pending |", "| **Date** | 2026-10-25 |", "| **Owners** | Accelerate Platform Retrieval + platform data governance |", "| **Related systems** | DataLake2 platform-owned publication, Accelerate dataset catalog, cross-deployment platform-data distribution |", "| **Canonical home (proposed)** | Accelerate retrieval specifications after approval; this DataLake2 copy is the working draft |", "", "## 1. Purpose", "", "Polaris already distinguishes tenant-owned data from platform-owned data. Platform-owned data includes corpora such", "as commercial news, SEC filings, licensed feeds, and other reference data that Polaris operates independently of a", "single client's standing or operation scope.", "", "The missing case is **platform-owned data that is not available to every otherwise eligible client**. A supplier or", "customer may provide a dataset that Polaris is permitted to operate as platform data, but only a named pool of", "clients may consume it.", "", "This document defines that distribution, retrieval, and event-participation policy:", "", "1. how a platform-owned dataset declares whether it is broadly available or requires explicit client grants;", "2. how client-to-dataset grants are administered, bound, enforced, audited, and revoked;", "3. how authorized clients use shared data without changing the shared record's ownership or conflating origin and", "   tenant canonical histories;", "4. how a dataset can later be replicated to a different AWS deployment without weakening its audience policy;", "5. how one dataset may participate in more than one deployment-local event pool without merging those pools or", "   confusing the dataset's storage scope with an event identity boundary; and", "6. which decisions remain contractual or product choices.", "", "It does **not** define how bytes arrive. S3, SFTP, Snowflake, API intake, scanning, normalization, and ledger design", "belong to platform-owned ingestion and source-specific adapter work. This design starts after a platform-owned", "pipeline has established a canonical dataset and its records carry the ordinary platform owner and provenance.", "", "## 2. Decision summary", "", "1. **Do not add a third owner type.** Restricted shared data remains `scope_owner = PLATFORM`. It is not tenant", "   data, a synthetic shared tenant, or a new `SHARED` scope.", "2. **Separate ownership, audience, and event identity.** `platform_scope_id` identifies a platform boundary for", "   provenance, isolation, and the destruction epoch. Audience, eligibility, security, retention, and licensing", "   policy belong to `dataset_id`. Separately, the typed scope of a set of canonical event rows serves as its event", "   pool: the local universe in which contributions may create, merge with, or enrich those events. A pool has no", "   second identifier. Neither the scope nor the pool is an audience list.", "3. **Authorize at dataset grain.** `dataset_id` is the first-release distribution unit. A restricted platform", "   dataset requires an explicit client-to-dataset entitlement. If two record populations require different client", "   audiences, use two datasets unless a real contractual requirement proves record-level audiences are necessary.", "4. **Entitlement is not eligibility.** Entitlement answers *which client may access this dataset*. Eligibility tags", "   answer *which approved product or reasoning use may consume it*. Security labels answer *which cleared viewer", "   may see a record or field*. A read must satisfy all three.", "5. **Platform Retrieval is the only client read path.** Clients and agents do not receive a platform principal or", "   direct database access. Retrieval binds the authenticated client to its effective dataset set, applies policy", "   before candidate selection, and rechecks it before delivery.", "6. **Client work derived from shared data remains client-owned.** An annotation, assessment, entity candidate,", "   accepted link, workflow state, or operation-specific interpretation belongs to that client's scope. It may", "   reference the shared record while access is valid, but it never mutates or changes ownership of the platform", "   record.", "7. **An entitlement does not move data between deployments.** Same-deployment access is an authorization change.", "   Access from another AWS account or isolated enclave requires an authorized replica through the separate", "   platform-data distribution contract.", "8. **Use a durable grant authority in Accelerate.** Only restricted platform datasets use explicit client grants.", "   Tenant-owned datasets keep their existing implicit `client_id` and standing/operation boundary; broad platform", "   datasets do not need per-client grants.", "9. **Keep V1 queries separate but use the same Retrieval verb.** Retrieval runs one server-constrained call for", "   the tenant/operation scope and another for authorized shared datasets. Mixed cross-scope ranking is deferred.", "10. **Attach narrow context; do not contaminate a broad canonical event.** A restricted platform artifact or", "    tenant-scoped artifact may enrich a shared event through an independently authorized, version-addressed", "    attachment. It cannot change the parent event's canonical fields, identity, merge result, search material, or", "    shared scoring. Material that must change those things requires a canonical object with a compatible audience.", "11. **A dataset is not an event pool.** One dataset or declared output class may participate in several pools. A", "    versioned, deployment-local binding gives each `(dataset, output class, target pool)` its own `SOURCED` or", "    `ENRICHES` role. For locally originated datasets, the scope-default `SOURCED` route is implicit once an event-", "    producing output class is active; imported replicas have no implicit route. Every non-default route requires a", "    current binding. Payloads never choose the pool or role.", "12. **Cross-account synthesis creates local truth, not a second platform authority.** Shared commercial remains", "    authoritative for its platform events. A destination imports authorized immutable platform evidence and event", "    references, then may run independent synthesis inside its tenant pools. The resulting tenant events are local", "    canonical objects with lineage to\u2014not continuations or mutations of\u2014the origin platform event.", "13. **A platform event pool publishes one derived event dataset.** Multi-source event rows use that dataset's", "    audience, eligibility, security, and lifecycle policy for serving. Contributor datasets remain exact lineage and", "    must each be at least as permissive as the derived dataset before `SOURCED` participation activates.", "", "## 3. Ownership, dataset, and audience are different axes", "", "### 3.1 Ownership classes", "", "| Data class | Canonical owner | Visibility rule |", "|---|---|---|", "| Tenant standing or operation data | `TENANT` + `client_id` and exact standing/operation scope | Only the authorized client scope |", "| Broad platform dataset | `PLATFORM` + `platform_scope_id` | Every client viewer allowed by dataset eligibility, security policy, and consumer policy |", "| Restricted platform dataset | `PLATFORM` + `platform_scope_id` | Only explicitly entitled clients that also satisfy eligibility and security policy |", "", "The final two rows use the same canonical ownership model. Their difference is dataset audience policy, not how", "their records are owned.", "", "Tenant-owned datasets do not participate in the client-to-platform-dataset grant mechanism. Their client audience", "is already implied by canonical `client_id` ownership and the exact standing/operation scope selected by the", "trusted Retrieval path.", "", "### 3.2 What each identifier means", "", "- `platform_scope_id` is the platform isolation and destruction-epoch key; it is a merge universe only for the", "  canonical event rows of a scope that is an event pool. Commercial news and SEC filings use different source", "  scopes because their provenance and source governance differ; the record's `dataset_id` is its serving-policy", "  owner for audience, eligibility, security, retention, destruction, and licensing. Distinct", "  source scopes do not by themselves prohibit declared outputs from contributing to the same event pool. For the", "  initial commercial rollout, the existing commercial-news event scope is retained as that pool's stable scope id", "  and its registry meaning is broadened to **shared commercial events**. This is a scope-registry", "  reclassification, not an event-identity rewrite or source-scope migration.", "- `dataset_id` identifies the registered feed or corpus to which a record belongs. It is the V1 distribution grain.", "- the event pool is the deployment-local typed scope whose canonical events form one merge universe. It is not a", "  dataset, audience, cross-deployment identity, or caller-selectable routing value; no second pool UUID duplicates", "  the scope identity.", "- `client_id` identifies the client receiving access. It is never copied onto the platform record and does not", "  become an owner of that record.", "- `operation_id` may scope a client's derived work, but it is never written onto the shared platform record.", "", "`platform_scope_id` is therefore not an audience list, and `dataset_id` is not a security principal. It remains a", "canonical data-policy boundary, while the event pool is the merge boundary. Inputs that participate in one event", "must still have audience and policy compatible with the resulting event. A narrower-audience artifact may cross", "into a broader pool only through the non-mutating attachment contract below; it does not become a canonical", "contribution to the broader event.", "", "### 3.3 Event pools and dataset participation", "", "An event pool is a first-class **logical** contract identified by the existing typed scope of the canonical events", "it owns. No parallel pool UUID or pool hierarchy is introduced:", "", "- a tenant standing pool maps to exactly one `(client_id, STANDING)` scope;", "- a tenant operation pool maps to exactly one `(client_id, OPERATION, operation_id)` scope;", "- a platform canonical pool is exactly one deliberately provisioned platform scope and carries its compatible", "  audience/policy boundary. Its event rows carry that pool's own `platform_scope_id`, which equals a contributor's", "  source scope only on that contributor's default route.", "  The pool registers its canonical-event output as one derived platform dataset with its own audience mode,", "  security floor, eligibility tags/cutoffs, retention/destruction, and licensing treatment; every event row carries", "  that derived `dataset_id`. The platform event scope and derived event dataset are enforced one-to-one. For event", "  rows, the dataset is the sole serving-policy owner; the scope remains the merge/isolation and destruction-epoch", "  key.", "", "A provisioned platform source scope does not automatically become an event pool. It does so only when a locally", "originated event-producing output class targets it, at which point the one derived event dataset is mandatory.", "A source-only platform scope has no derived event dataset and no implicit event route.", "", "A tenant standing or operation pool that receives any non-default platform `SOURCED` binding must first register", "one tenant-owned event publication dataset for that pool. Its current events receive that dataset id before the", "binding activates, and later event rows carry it. That dataset is the serving-policy owner for every event in the", "pool. Registration validates every currently effective route into the pool\u2014including first-party default routes\u2014", "and every dataset in current event lineage against its policy; any failure blocks registration. A first-party", "output class activated later is checked against that policy when it activates. The dataset owns the pool's", "eligibility tags/cutoffs, security floor, licensing, and lifecycle treatment used by the anti-laundering", "compatibility check. A pool using only its own first-party default route does not need this additional publication", "dataset.", "", "A read-only destination replica of an origin platform event is **not** a local event pool: it accepts no local", "contributions and has no local merge authority. It is origin truth available for Retrieval, correlation, and as", "lineage-bearing input to separately authorized destination pools.", "", "Wave 1 remains simple: every active event-producing output class from a locally originated dataset resolves to its", "typed scope's default local event pool, so the shared-spine work does not require operators to create arbitrary pool", "identifiers or add one binding per operation. A source-only scope with no active event-producing output class has no", "event pool. Non-default bindings are required only before a dataset is allowed to affect another pool or a", "distributed replica is used in destination-local synthesis.", "", "The implicit default applies only to a dataset originated and canonical in this deployment. A dataset imported as", "a distribution replica has no implicit default route and cannot be `SOURCED` into a destination platform pool. Its", "portable contributions participate only through explicit destination tenant-pool bindings; absent such a binding,", "they produce no destination event.", "", "A versioned **event-participation binding** authorizes one declared dataset output class to act in one target pool", "or trusted pool selector:", "", "```text", "dataset_id + output_class + target_pool_selector -> event_role + policy_version", "```", "", "The only active roles are:", "", "| Role | Meaning |", "|---|---|", "| `SOURCED` | The declared output may create a new event or change canonical event state inside the target pool |", "| `ENRICHES` | The output may create an authorized attachment/correlation but cannot create an event or change canonical parent state |", "", "In V1, the source dataset of a non-default binding must be `scope_owner = PLATFORM`, whether locally originated or", "an imported replica. A tenant-owned dataset participates only through its scope-default route. Connecting tenant", "standing and operation scopes remains an F-ING-12 governed cross-scope association, never an event-participation", "binding or canonical synthesis route.", "", "For an output class declared to produce events, the scope-default `SOURCED` route is implicit and needs no row. An", "explicit `SOURCED` binding to another platform pool replaces that default platform target; it does not duplicate", "it. Each `(dataset_id, output_class)` has at most one effective `SOURCED` platform target per deployment.", "Additional `SOURCED` targets may exist in distinct tenant standing/operation scopes because those are deliberately", "separate canonical histories, not competing platform truth. Every `ENRICHES` target and every non-default", "`SOURCED` target requires a current binding; absence, expiry, or revocation means no non-default participation. V1", "does not need a stored `NONE` role.", "", "The role is per binding, not a property of the dataset. A public filing dataset can therefore be `SOURCED` in a", "shared platform pool, `ENRICHES` in selected tenant pools, and absent everywhere else. Another approved product may", "bind the same dataset as `SOURCED` in an entitled tenant's standing pool so the destination maintains its own", "complete local event universe.", "", "Bindings may name a specific pool or a trusted selector such as \u201cthe standing pool for each client entitled to", "this dataset.\u201d Operation pools require an explicit, attributed binding to the named operation; V1 has no open-ended", "operation selector, and a broad grant never silently fans data into every operation. The resolver expands permitted", "standing selectors from server-side deployment, entitlement, and typed-scope state. An envelope, artifact, model,", "agent, or client caller cannot supply or override the target scope, selector, or role.", "", "Selector expansion is pinned before work begins. The plan/ledger records the exact binding version and resolved", "target typed scope; retries and replay reuse those values. A later client grant, operation, or pool does not", "silently receive historical contributions. Any catch-up/backfill is a separately planned, bounded act under the", "then-current binding and entitlement.", "", "Entitlement-driven selectors apply only to `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS` datasets. An `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` dataset", "requires an explicit per-client binding before it may participate in a tenant pool; broad retrieval eligibility", "never implies corpus-wide synthesis into every client. Adding a client to a selector starts forward-only", "participation. Historical backfill is a separately attributed, bounded job with its own approval and capacity", "check.", "", "Commercial news and SEC filings illustrate the separation. Their records retain different platform scopes and", "dataset identities, while declared event outputs from both may be `SOURCED` into one shared-platform event scope.", "For the initial commercial rollout, that event pool reuses the existing commercial-news event scope and stable", "`platform_scope_id`; the scope registry names its event-pool purpose **shared commercial events**. News records", "keep their current source scope. SEC records keep their separate SEC source scope, and SEC event outputs use an", "explicit `SOURCED` binding into the shared pool before publication. Existing news event UUIDs and history stay in", "place; there is no second pool, dual write, event-identity migration, or wholesale re-synthesis. Existing clean", "platform-news rows receive the additive pool-scope and derived-event-dataset policy pointers; legacy contributions", "that are tenant-origin or cannot be proven platform-owned follow the bounded cleanup gate in Section 12.1. The", "event rows carry the shared-events scope's `platform_scope_id`; each contribution retains its own source scope and", "dataset provenance. Their evidence does not collapse, but ESS may synthesize one event when both describe the same", "occurrence. Every effective `SOURCED` route\u2014including the implicit scope default\u2014is valid only", "when the target event publication dataset's audience, security floor, eligibility, retention/destruction, and", "licensing treatment are no broader than every source input it admits. Every route is validated when it becomes", "effective. A default route is validated when its event-producing output class activates, including for a dataset", "added to an already provisioned scope; a non-default route is validated at binding activation. A failed check", "blocks activation. Otherwise the input is limited to `ENRICHES` or rejected. Merge eligibility comes from the", "effective route and target policy, never merely from equal or different source scopes.", "", "The derived platform event dataset is the Retrieval and policy authority for multi-source events; an event is not", "assigned a fictional base source item or one contributor's dataset. For a tenant standing or operation pool, the", "comparison target is the tenant event publication dataset carried by that pool's canonical events. A platform", "dataset may be `SOURCED` into the tenant pool only when that publication dataset's eligibility tags/cutoffs,", "security floor, licensing, and lifecycle treatment are no broader than the source; otherwise the route is", "`ENRICHES`-only. Event lineage still names every contributing dataset and exact source version for revocation,", "explanation, and destruction.", "", "### 3.4 Shared parent with audience-scoped attachments", "", "One broad canonical event may have independently governed context attached to it:", "", "```text", "shared canonical event", "  \u251c\u2500\u2500 broad platform attachment", "  \u251c\u2500\u2500 restricted platform attachment  (dataset grant required)", "  \u2514\u2500\u2500 tenant/operation attachment      (exact client scope required)", "```", "", "This reuses the existing research-packet and client-scoped-artifact semantics rather than copying or re-versioning", "the parent for every audience. Attachments are `ENRICHES`-only. An attachment points to an exact artifact version,", "and the relationship and target are visible only when the viewer is authorized to both endpoints; otherwise", "Retrieval exposes no edge, count, marker, handle, citation, or metadata proving it exists.", "", "`ENRICHES` adds separately retrievable judgment-relevant context and", "must not alter the shared event's title, summary, classification, entities, materiality, status, canonical search", "text or embeddings, merge identity, or other parent metadata. Client-specific cards, annotations, and judgments", "remain separately scoped derivatives, not mutations of the event.", "", "Material that helps establish the event is an ordinary contribution admitted under a `SOURCED` binding, never an", "attachment. Its audience therefore cannot be narrower than the event it changes. When restricted material is", "necessary to establish the event or any canonical event field, it must produce a separately restricted canonical", "event/object in an audience-compatible pool. V1 does not implement field-by-field redaction inside one merged", "canonical event.", "", "The existing attachment tables are a semantic precedent, not automatically a sufficient physical security", "boundary. The implementation must use an audience-aware link family (or extend the generalized attachment", "contract) that carries enough physical scope and target identity for store-enforced authorization and direct-table", "negative tests.", "", "Participation bindings govern pipeline-produced dataset output classes only. Client-authored research packets,", "assessments, notes, and other scoped artifacts use their existing tenant artifact workflow and the two-endpoint", "authorization rule; they need no dataset-output binding and are always `ENRICHES`-only.", "", "### 3.5 Supplier provenance is not canonical ownership", "", "A customer or partner may be the supplier without being the canonical security owner. The platform record retains", "supplier identity, agreement/license references, permitted-use terms, retention/destruction obligations, and", "source provenance. `scope_owner = PLATFORM` means Polaris operates the canonical authorization boundary; it does", "not claim Polaris owns the supplier's intellectual property.", "", "The contributing client receives no implicit read grant merely because it supplied the source. Its access is", "administered by the same entitlement rule as every other client.", "", "Data already accepted as tenant-owned is never silently promoted into platform ownership. The legal/governance", "decision that a source is platform-owned must be made before it enters the platform-owned publication path.", "", "## 4. Dataset audience policy", "", "Every active platform dataset has an explicit, versioned audience-policy record attached to its stable", "`dataset_id`. The policy is independent of immutable descriptive dataset declaration versions so authorization can", "change without rewriting dataset provenance. Missing or unknown policy fails activation.", "", "The physical authority keeps current state small and version history immutable:", "", "- `ingest.dataset_audience_policies` has one row per platform dataset, keyed by `dataset_id`, with nullable", "  `current_policy_version_id` and monotonic `authorization_epoch`. This is the only current pointer/epoch row.", "- Immutable policy versions carry `id`, `dataset_id`, audience mode, derivative ceiling, canonical digest, and the", "  approved effective contract. A composite foreign key proves the current pointer belongs to the same dataset.", "- Append-only `dataset_audience_policy_facts` record policy changes, epoch-only invalidations, exact prior/new", "  versions, attribution, and emitted-event identity. Facts are audit/evidence, not the current lookup surface.", "", "The dataset reader receives only the stable row's `dataset_id`, `current_policy_version_id`, and", "`authorization_epoch` plus the immutable version's `id`, `dataset_id`, `audience_mode`, and `canonical_digest`.", "It cannot read raw policy facts or operator attribution. This is sufficient for server-side binding and stale-read", "invalidation without turning the policy history into client-facing catalog metadata.", "", "| Audience mode | Meaning |", "|---|---|", "| `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` | Any client viewer may use the dataset if its consumer policy, eligibility requirements, and security floor permit it |", "| `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS` | The client must additionally hold a current entitlement to this exact `dataset_id` |", "", "There is no default from `PLATFORM` ownership to broad client availability. Dataset activation must choose the", "mode deliberately and record the responsible authority, effective time, policy version, and the maximum", "contract-permitted derivative treatment. It also records the treatment applied to clients that lose access without", "ever having an explicit grant, such as when a broad dataset becomes restricted. Missing treatment fails activation.", "", "Changing `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` to `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS`, narrowing the audience contract, or narrowing the", "dataset security floor is a dataset-policy narrowing. It advances that dataset's authorization epoch. Eligibility", "tag/cutoff changes are different: current currency history applies them at read time, narrowing returns", "`RESTART_REQUIRED` to an open continuation, and no audience authorization epoch advances. Removing a grant or shortening its", "validity is a client-grant narrowing and advances the affected client's dataset-access revision. Retrieval binds", "both identifiers and invalidates continuations and caches when either changes.", "", "Lowering a dataset's derivative ceiling atomically supersedes every grant whose pinned treatment is now too", "permissive. Effective treatment follows the single strictest-wins rule in Section 7.1, so lowering the dataset", "ceiling takes effect without waiting for an operator to repair stale grants.", "", "Adding a grant or changing `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS` to `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` widens access. Widening always", "requires attributed human approval tied to the supplier agreement and the runbook's exposure-accounting step;", "automation may prepare but must not silently approve it.", "", "The audience policy is independent of:", "", "- dataset eligibility tags such as `general_retrieval` or `signal_pattern_judgment_eligible`;", "- dataset grouping, collection, topic, or descriptive metadata;", "- artifact type, document type, source-system identity, and supplier identity; and", "- record/field labels and viewer clearance.", "", "None of those fields grants client access.", "", "## 5. Client-to-dataset entitlement", "", "### 5.1 Logical contract", "", "The authoritative decision is equivalent to:", "", "```text", "client_id may access dataset_id", "from effective_at until optional expires_at", "under grant policy/version and attributed authority", "```", "", "Accelerate owns a small durable grant authority behind Platform Retrieval's entitlement-authority seam. It applies", "only to `PLATFORM + EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS`; it is not a parallel authorization system for tenant-owned or broad", "platform datasets. A future enterprise authorization service may write through or replace this authority only if", "it preserves the same stable interface, history, revision, and fail-closed behavior.", "", "At minimum, grant history preserves:", "", "- the globally allocated `client_id` and stable UUID `dataset_id`;", "- grant, revoke, supersession, effective, and optional expiry times;", "- the actor or authority responsible for the decision;", "- the policy/agreement reference supporting the decision;", "- the exact derivative treatment permitted for this client, never more permissive than the dataset policy;", "- a reason or protected operator reference; and", "- the resulting client dataset-access revision.", "", "No grant activates without an explicit derivative treatment. The grant authority maintains a monotonic,", "deployment-local `dataset_access_revision` for each client. Any grant change that affects the client's effective", "dataset set increments that revision atomically with the grant-state change.", "", "Grant time bounds are enforced directly, not inferred from a revision. Every effective-scope bind and final", "delivery recheck evaluates `effective_at <= now < expires_at` when an expiry exists. A scheduled expiry processor", "later appends the attributed terminal transition, increments the client revision, and starts derivative cleanup,", "but a late or failed processor can never extend access beyond `expires_at`.", "", "Do not store `allowed_client_ids[]` on datasets or content rows. Entitlements need attributed history, efficient", "revocation, and independent administration; an embedded array provides none of those cleanly.", "", "### 5.2 Grant evaluation", "", "A restricted platform record is visible only when all of the following are true:", "", "1. the authenticated viewer resolves server-side to the entitled `client_id`;", "2. that client has a current grant for the record's exact `dataset_id`;", "3. the dataset is active and current under the request-bound catalog revision;", "4. the consumer policy permits the retrieval type and intended use;", "5. the dataset carries every eligibility tag required by that consumer at the bound effective cutoff;", "6. the viewer satisfies the dataset and record/field security floor; and", "7. the grant's effective/expiry bounds, client dataset-access revision, dataset authorization epoch, and viewer", "   authorization remain current through final delivery.", "", "The caller never supplies or overrides the effective `client_id`, platform scope, dataset grant set, entitlement,", "database role, or row predicate.", "", "### 5.3 Administration", "", "Grants are deployment-local administrative state. A reviewed operator or future approval workflow may create,", "expire, or revoke them. Every grant creation, validity extension, grant re-enable, or other audience widening", "requires attributed human approval and exposure accounting. This is distinct from \u00a712.1's operational", "`SERVING_DISABLED -> ACTIVE` recovery under an unchanged policy. Automation may prepare a proposed change but", "never approves it.", "", "Real customer grant manifests, agreement details, data samples, and credentials remain deployment-local data.", "They do not enter source repositories, branches, pull requests, or ordinary CI fixtures.", "", "## 6. Retrieval enforcement", "", "### 6.1 Effective scope", "", "Platform Retrieval resolves the viewer's authorized dataset set and includes it in the trusted effective-scope", "manifest together with the current client `dataset_access_revision`, each admitted dataset's authorization epoch,", "and the grant time bounds. Those values are also bound into cursors, freezes, page references, caches, and", "recorded-response authorization context. For a tenant viewer, the logical row floor is:", "", "```text", "tenant branch:", "  tenant-owned row matches the viewer's client and exact standing/operation scope", "", "OR", "", "platform branch:", "  platform-owned row belongs to a platform dataset whose audience policy admits this client", "  and the viewer, consumer, eligibility, and security checks all pass", "```", "", "For a canonical platform event, that `dataset_id` is the event pool's registered derived event dataset\u2014not one of", "its contributing source datasets. Contributor lineage remains independently available for policy invalidation,", "explanation, retraction, and destruction, but it does not replace the row's serving-policy owner.", "", "This extends the current launch posture in which platform rows require an explicit platform principal and no", "consumer holds one. The client still never receives a platform principal. A trusted Retrieval execution path binds", "the client and authorized dataset set and enforces them in the authoritative store before text/vector top-K,", "faceting, aggregation, traversal, hydration, agent context, or LLM input.", "", "The complete Retrieval context contains `retrieval.client_id`, `retrieval.operation_ids`,", "`retrieval.include_standing`, and `retrieval.platform_dataset_ids`. Platform Retrieval's row-floor helper is the", "only writer of the client and sets it transaction-locally before protected work. The protected audience/grant", "lookup owned by UNI-22066 uses that client to derive the permitted platform dataset ids; it cannot require the", "allow-list it is computing. DAT-11100's checked binder validates the existing client and writes only the operation", "array, standing Boolean, and platform-dataset array before ordinary catalog or canonical-record reads. The UUID", "arrays are typed, sorted, and deduplicated. The platform allow-list deliberately carries dataset ids rather than", "`platform_scope_id`: one platform scope may contain datasets with different audiences. Scope-bearing record", "policies consume all four settings. Owner-scoped catalog policies do not use operation ids or the standing flag:", "tenant rows use the client, while platform rows require an exact bound dataset and the durable audience/grant", "policy. Missing, malformed, contradictory, or unbound required context admits no row; standing is included only for", "the exact Boolean true. Retrieval calls no `pio.*` binder. Writer policies continue to use that separate typed", "ingest-scope context.", "", "The implementation may use RLS, security-barrier functions/views, or an equivalently reviewed store-enforced", "mechanism, but an application-only `WHERE dataset_id IN (...)` is not sufficient as the sole security boundary.", "The store predicate resolves the server-bound `client_id` against the durable grant authority and audience-policy", "tables; it does not trust a caller- or application-supplied dataset list. Bound revisions and epochs detect stale", "reads rather than replace that authorization lookup.", "Before delivery, Retrieval rechecks the current time bounds, client dataset-access revision, and dataset", "authorization epochs. A stale value fails closed through the existing restart/refusal path. A dataset-policy change", "therefore invalidates affected reads without fan-out writes to every viewer identity.", "", "### 6.2 Discovery and metadata", "", "An unentitled client must not learn that a restricted dataset exists. The same audience rule protects:", "", "- dataset descriptions and catalog entries;", "- dataset-specific retrieval types or relationships;", "- live status, coverage, freshness, counts, and facets;", "- record handles, citations, edges, and source provenance;", "- validation diagnostics and authorization errors; and", "- aggregate behavior that would reveal the hidden dataset.", "", "Model-facing behavior must not distinguish \"dataset exists but you lack access\" from \"dataset is unavailable or", "does not exist.\"", "", "Client-facing catalog discovery never uses unrestricted direct catalog reads. Dataset descriptions, versions,", "currency policy, live status, and other metadata are exposed through RLS or a security-barrier read interface that", "uses the same server-bound client and audience predicate as record retrieval. The ingestion/catalog", "`ingest_dataset_reader` database role remains an internal service role; Platform Retrieval must not use its raw", "table access to answer a client-facing catalog request. This does not require another login role.", "", "V1 requires structural non-disclosure: response shape, status/error class, counts, facets, and identifiers must not", "vary in a way that confirms a hidden dataset. Statistical timing-side-channel resistance is outside V1; normal", "latency monitoring and threat review still apply.", "", "### 6.3 Authoritative and projected serving stores", "", "Phase A Postgres/Aurora enforcement applies audience policy through the authoritative store boundary before", "candidate selection. A later compiler or projected store, including a CDC-fed OpenSearch index, must not serve a", "restricted platform dataset until it can enforce the client's effective dataset set before top-K selection,", "faceting, counting, aggregation, and hydration, and it passes the same negative-access tests as Postgres.", "", "This design does not prematurely require a particular physical index layout. If a projected store cannot prove", "that pre-selection boundary, restricted datasets stay routed to Postgres. Filtering unauthorized candidates after", "OpenSearch has ranked or counted them is not compliant.", "", "### 6.4 First-release query posture", "", "The smallest safe first release keeps the existing Platform Retrieval query verb but executes separate", "server-constrained calls: one for the client's tenant/operation scope and one for its authorized shared datasets.", "The caller does not select arbitrary dataset IDs; Retrieval derives and binds the permitted set. An agent may", "correlate the separately retrieved evidence in its governed context.", "", "A single ranked query mixing tenant and platform datasets is compatible with this design, but is not required for", "V1. It adds ranking, explanation, count, paging, and leakage questions and should ship only when a product use case", "requires it and the Retrieval contract proves those behaviors.", "", "## 7. Client-derived work and cross-scope links", "", "Client-specific work over shared data remains tenant-owned:", "", "- a client's note, assessment, classification, or workflow state is stored in its standing/operation scope;", "- entity candidates and unresolved alternatives remain scoped processing state;", "- only qualified or adjudicated links become canonical client-scoped relationships;", "- the shared platform artifact, entity, or event is never mutated, re-owned, or made the same canonical row as a", "  tenant object merely because a client used it; and", "- reading a client-to-platform relationship requires authorization to both endpoints at read time. If the platform", "  target is no longer authorized, Retrieval exposes no dangling target, count, or descriptive metadata.", "", "Every tenant-owned derivative of a shared record carries machine-queryable lineage to the source `dataset_id` and", "stable source record/version identity. That lineage is protected from ordinary client output but retained for", "authorization checks, revocation cleanup, audit, and destruction receipts.", "", "This permits an Apollo operation, for example, to link its investigation-specific entity or observation to an", "authorized shared reference record without making that shared record part of the operation or exposing Apollo's", "work to another entitled client.", "", "The same rule applies to event processing in both directions:", "", "- A tenant-scoped research packet, evidence item, or assessment may attach to a broad platform event as", "  `ENRICHES`. It never changes that platform event.", "- An authorized platform contribution may be processed under a destination-local `SOURCED` binding and influence", "  a distinct tenant event inside that tenant's pool. The contribution retains platform ownership and exact origin", "  lineage; the resulting event and its derived fields are tenant-owned. This is independent synthesis over shared", "  evidence, not a cross-owner canonical merge.", "- Under an `ENRICHES` binding, imported platform material may attach or correlate to a tenant event but cannot", "  change its canonical fields or create a tenant event.", "", "The logical processing slot is `(contribution identity, target typed event scope)`. Delivery dedupe remains the", "`DESIGN-data-ingestion.md` \u00a74.4 version-attempt and \u00a74.17 operation identity; the binding version is recorded as", "provenance and never mints a second slot. A binding-version change applies prospectively; existing contributions", "retain the version recorded in their lineage. An existing contribution is retracted/recomputed only when it no", "longer satisfies current authority", "(`SOURCED` to `ENRICHES`, revocation, or Section 8 policy incompatibility). Promoting historical `ENRICHES` material", "to `SOURCED` is a separate bounded backfill. For a replacement, the router deterministically mints the new", "generation for that logical slot from the binding transition; it is not borrowed from an upstream `run_id`.", "Non-default processing derives its pool-qualified raw-event slot from the contribution identity and target scope,", "so the shared spine's `raw_event_id` ledger and receipt uniqueness remain unchanged. The same immutable", "contribution may lawfully be processed once into a shared platform pool and again into one or more authorized", "tenant pools without the attempts colliding or one pool's result becoming another pool's canonical history.", "", "On a non-default route, the pool-qualified raw-event receipt, canonical event, retraction targets, and directly", "queryable event link rows carry the **target pool's** typed scope, and `DESIGN-data-ingestion.md` \u00a74.17 scope", "equality applies among those event-side objects. The contributing source item keeps its own **source scope**. Its", "provenance link references that exact source item and effective route as validated lineage; it does not assert", "source-scope equals target-pool scope on a non-default route. Wave 1 retains the same-scope composite FK for its", "default-only route. The later event-participation implementation atomically adds the binding reference and replaces", "that FK with a database-enforced rule allowing either exact scope equality or a PLATFORM-owned source authorized by", "the pinned non-default binding; tenant-to-platform, tenant-to-other-tenant, and cross-operation links always fail.", "A non-default route records its pinned binding version; a default route records the implicit scope default and no", "binding row. Work", "carries and ESS checks the current destruction epochs for both the source scope and target pool, quarantining the", "operation if either stamp is stale. On the default route, source scope equals target pool scope and the existing", "contract is unchanged.", "", "An audience-aware attachment is independently searchable and citable only for an authorized viewer. Revocation", "hides the attachment and relationship without rewriting an unaffected parent event. A tenant event whose canonical", "state was derived under a `SOURCED` binding follows the effective treatment below rather than pretending its source", "disappeared from lineage.", "", "### 7.1 Platform-derived outputs", "", "A platform-owned aggregate, signal, embedding, classification, or other output derived from restricted inputs keeps", "lineage to every contributing dataset and may have no audience broader than the intersection of those inputs'", "audiences. If the producing pipeline and Retrieval cannot preserve and enforce that inherited audience, the output", "must not be produced from restricted inputs or published to a shared surface.", "", "This includes computations over a shared event plus one of its narrow attachments. The broad event may remain", "visible, but any result whose content or score was influenced by the attachment inherits the attachment's audience", "and lives as a separate governed output. It may not overwrite a broadly visible score or summary on the parent.", "", "The dataset audience policy defines the most permissive post-revocation derivative treatment allowed by the", "supplier agreement. Each client grant pins an exact treatment at or below that ceiling.", "The effective treatment is always the strictest of the source dataset ceiling, distribution-subscription maximum,", "client grant (or the dataset's ungranted-revocation treatment), and any binding restriction. A binding may only", "narrow treatment; it never widens another authority. Autonomous `SOURCED` synthesis is always source-derived and", "therefore retracts/recomputes under Section 8 when its source authority is lost.", "", "Under the approved default:", "", "- genuine client-authored judgment or conclusions may remain when the supplier agreement permits;", "- copied source text, source-derived summaries, embeddings and search projections, citations, record handles, and", "  anything else that reconstructs or exposes the source must be removed or made non-dereferenceable; and", "- stricter agreements may require hiding/tombstoning the derived relationship or destroying the derivative and", "  retaining only the required audit/destruction receipt.", "", "Merely running source material through an LLM or classifier does not turn the output into independent", "client-authored judgment. Autonomous summaries, embeddings, classifications, and extracted facts remain", "source-derived. A conclusion qualifies for retention only when an authorized client user authored it or explicitly", "adopted it through an attributed workflow action and it does not reproduce or substitute for the source.", "", "## 8. Revocation and contract change", "", "Revoking or narrowing a client entitlement:", "", "1. appends an attributed grant-state change;", "2. atomically advances the affected client's dataset-access revision, which the existing viewer authorization", "   epoch seam consumes;", "3. removes the dataset from new effective-scope manifests and metadata projections;", "4. invalidates old cursors, page references, freezes, caches, recorded responses, and pending delivery;", "5. blocks citation resolution, relationship hydration, replay, handle re-fetch, and new event-pool processing;", "6. retracts the dataset's exact `SOURCED` contributions and `ENRICHES` links from that client's tenant pools only", "   through the ordinary versioned retraction/recompute path\u2014surviving evidence may sustain the event, while an", "   unsupported event is tombstoned rather than silently retaining revoked influence; and", "7. leaves the canonical origin platform dataset/event intact unless the supplier agreement separately requires", "   retirement or destruction.", "", "Revoking a participation binding without revoking retrieval access performs the same pool-local retraction for", "that binding only. Revoking an origin distribution subscription additionally stops import, invalidates destination", "bindings, and applies its pinned retention/destruction policy to the replica and every source-derived projection.", "Cleanup is idempotent, pool-qualified, attributable, and receipt-producing; hiding a record from Retrieval alone is", "not sufficient when it previously influenced canonical tenant state.", "", "A client-grant change never retracts from a platform pool or another client's pool. Those pools change only when", "their own binding, source contribution, dataset policy, or distribution subscription changes. Retention expiry,", "supplier-required destruction, or retirement of a `SOURCED` contribution triggers the same pool-qualified", "retract/recompute/tombstone path in **every** pool it influenced, including a pool owned by another platform scope.", "No summary, embedding, score, or canonical field may silently retain destroyed-source influence.", "", "Narrowing a source dataset's audience, security floor, eligibility, retention, destruction, or licensing policy", "re-evaluates every pool holding a live contribution from it\u2014through a current binding, the implicit default, or a", "retired/retargeted binding\u2014as part of the same approved change. An effective route that no longer satisfies Section", "3.3 is suspended before new contributions are admitted, then its existing contributions run the pool-qualified", "retract/recompute/tombstone path. The only alternative is to narrow the target event publication dataset's policy", "in that same approved change so it remains no broader than every source. Widening a platform event dataset is", "rejected while any dataset in its live contribution lineage is narrower.", "", "Retargeting a `SOURCED` binding does not move or rewrite existing canonical events. The old pool stops receiving", "new contributions and retains its attributed history unless policy requires retraction. Any historical synthesis", "into the new pool is a separate, bounded, attributed job; correlation may connect the old and new canonical events", "without merging their histories.", "", "Client-derived facts created while access was valid do not become shared data. Their treatment follows the exact", "grant policy described in Section 7. A client that had access under `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` and therefore has no", "pinned grant uses the dataset audience policy's required ungranted-revocation treatment. The system must not infer", "that policy from dataset eligibility tags.", "", "## 9. Cross-deployment distribution seam", "", "An entitlement is valid only in the deployment that holds both the dataset and the grant. It is not a remote read", "credential and does not move records.", "", "If an entitled client is hosted in another AWS account, region, or isolated enclave:", "", "1. the origin authorizes a dataset subscription under the separate platform-data distribution contract, including", "   the permitted destination deployment and client IDs, output classes, maximum derivative treatment, and whether", "   broad mode or local event participation is allowed;", "2. the origin sends a versioned snapshot followed by ordered immutable changes, each identified by global UUIDs.", "   The dependency closure includes the authorized dataset records, evidence/artifact versions, portable", "   contribution facts, provenance, and any origin canonical-event snapshots/references required by the product;", "3. the destination imports idempotently into a local read-only platform replica and dataset catalog without", "   changing origin ownership or canonical identity;", "4. the destination verifies and activates the local dataset replica; only then may it create or activate its own", "   deployment-local client entitlement and event-participation bindings; and", "5. Retrieval and synthesis use only destination-local copies, bindings, pools, and authorization state.", "", "The origin and destination have different authorities:", "", "- The origin is the sole writer of the origin platform event. A destination stores its replica/reference as", "  read-only and applies ordered origin changes; it does not run that row back through ESS and mint a competing", "  platform-canonical history.", "- The destination may independently process an authorized portable contribution into one or more local tenant", "  pools. Each pool produces a distinct local canonical event or attachment according to its own binding. The", "  local result records the origin deployment, dataset, contribution, artifact/event version, and distribution", "  subscription lineage.", "- An imported replica has no implicit destination-platform `SOURCED` route. Without an explicit tenant-pool", "  binding, its portable contributions create no destination event.", "- One imported platform copy can serve several locally entitled clients; physical duplication per client is not", "  required unless the deployment's isolation architecture requires it. Each client's pool processing and output", "  remain isolated and independently revocable.", "- Nothing derived from a tenant pool flows back into the origin platform pool by default. Any future release or", "  declassification path is a separately approved publication workflow, not replication acknowledgement.", "", "The target event scope is deployment-local and is never matched across accounts. Cross-deployment identity uses the", "origin deployment identifier plus global dataset, contribution, artifact/version, and canonical-event UUIDs. The", "destination mints new UUIDs for its local tenant events and carries explicit lineage/correlation to origin objects.", "The logical destination slot is `(origin contribution identity, destination target scope)`. Import dedupe is at", "least `(subscription, origin change identity, destination object)`, while local delivery uses the ordinary", "version-attempt, operation identity, and new-generation replacement contract for that logical slot. The binding", "version is retained as lineage; changing it does not manufacture a second slot.", "", "A planned or active origin subscription is not a client entitlement. A destination must never hold an active grant", "for a dataset replica that is absent or inactive locally, and must reject any audience policy or grant broader than", "the origin subscription permits.", "", "An origin canonical-event replica is imported only under a subscription to the origin pool's derived event dataset.", "The destination serves it only after that dataset's local replica is active with an audience policy no broader than", "the subscription. Contributor lineage on the event replica authorizes cleanup and explanation but exposes no", "contributor dataset, record, or metadata the viewer is not independently allowed to see.", "", "Database-local serials and watermarks, including `source_event_changes.id`, are never treated as globally unique", "identities. Snapshot/change handoff, ordering, replay, destruction, and subscription revocation belong to", "`DAT-9083`; that checkpoint must also specify target-scope binding/resolution, portable contribution closure,", "destination-local synthesis idempotency, origin-reference handling, and loop prevention before any distributor or", "importer is deployed.", "", "CUI never enters the commercial AWS account. A commercial environment may distribute explicitly approved non-CUI", "platform/reference data into an isolated environment, but an isolated client does not query the commercial", "database and commercial infrastructure never receives enclave data.", "", "## 10. Security invariants", "", "- No client can read another client's tenant-owned data.", "- Platform ownership alone never grants client visibility.", "- A restricted platform dataset is fail-closed when its audience policy, entitlement authority, or viewer binding", "  is unavailable or inconsistent.", "- Dataset entitlement, eligibility, and security clearance are separate conjunctive checks.", "- Entitlement is applied before discovery and candidate selection and rechecked before every delivery or replay.", "- Revisions supplement time checks; reaching `expires_at` denies access even if an expiry worker has not run.", "- No projected store may serve restricted data by filtering only after ranking, counting, or aggregation.", "- A supplier/contributor has no implicit entitlement.", "- No client or agent receives a platform principal, raw database credential, caller-selectable dataset grant,", "  target event pool, or event role.", "- Platform and tenant canonical records never become the same identity or share a canonical history. An explicitly", "  authorized platform contribution may be an input to independent tenant-pool synthesis while retaining its own", "  ownership and lineage.", "- A locally originated dataset affects only its implicit scope-default `SOURCED` pool unless a current deployment-", "  local binding authorizes a non-default target. An imported replica has no implicit route. Missing, expired, or", "  ambiguous non-default authority fails closed.", "- The same contribution processed in multiple pools has distinct pool-qualified ledger identity and cannot cause", "  cross-pool retry adoption, merge, or publication.", "- A destination cannot mutate origin platform truth, and destination tenant outputs never replicate back to the", "  origin without a separate approved release/declassification workflow.", "- A narrower-audience input never changes a broader canonical event; it is either a separately authorized", "  `ENRICHES` attachment or a separately restricted canonical object.", "- Every attachment traversal authorizes both endpoints before exposing the relationship or allowing the target to", "  influence ranking, counts, facets, hydration, agent context, or derived output.", "- Every grant, revoke, audience-mode change, replica subscription, distribution, and destruction act is attributed", "  and auditable.", "- Real client configuration and data remain outside source control.", "", "## 11. Acceptance scenarios", "", "1. Platform dataset D uses `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS`. Clients A and B are entitled; C is not. A and B can discover", "   and retrieve D under their ordinary viewer identities. C receives no metadata, result, count, edge, citation, or", "   distinguishable error revealing D.", "2. Platform dataset N uses `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS`. A viewer still fails when its consumer lacks the required", "   eligibility tag or its clearance fails the security floor. Broad audience is not public access.", "3. Dataset D carries `general_retrieval`, while a consumer requires `signal_pattern_judgment_eligible`. An entitled", "   client still cannot use D through that consumer. Eligibility cannot be replaced by entitlement.", "4. B's grant is revoked during a paged query. No later page, replay, citation resolution, edge hydration, or handle", "   re-fetch returns D; the changed dataset-access revision forces the existing restart/refusal outcome.", "5. A and B annotate the same shared record. Their annotations remain isolated tenant/operation records. Neither", "   client can see or affect the other's work, and the platform record remains unchanged.", "6. A tenant event and a platform event describe the same real-world occurrence. Retrieval may present both to an", "   authorized client, but ESS never merges their canonical histories.", "7. A client in another AWS account has an approved origin subscription but no active destination replica. No", "   destination grant may activate and the client reads nothing. After authorized distribution, import, and local", "   dataset activation, the destination may activate its local grant; no live commercial database dependency exists.", "8. A local serial collides between origin and destination. Global UUID identity and ordered distribution metadata", "   prevent conflation.", "9. The contributor's own grant expires. Contribution provenance remains, but the contributor loses access exactly", "   like any other client.", "10. A tenant-owned dataset needs no shared-dataset grant. Its existing `client_id` and standing/operation boundary", "    still isolate it, and creating or revoking a platform dataset grant cannot widen tenant data.", "11. A projected store contains restricted dataset D. Client C's query produces no D-derived top-K candidate,", "    count, facet, aggregate, citation, or hydration result. If this cannot be proven, D stays on", "    the Postgres serving path.", "12. B loses access under a grant that permits retention of genuine client judgment. B keeps its attributable", "    conclusion, but copied text, summaries, embeddings, projections, citations, handles, and source dereference are", "    removed. A stricter grant destroys or tombstones the derivative and records the required receipt.", "13. Changing D from restricted to broad or adding a new client grant cannot activate without attributed human", "    approval and exposure accounting.", "14. Repository scanning proves no real client grant manifest, supplier agreement, credential, supplier-provided", "    schema or data dictionary, or data sample was committed.", "15. B's grant expires between page one and page two while the scheduled expiry processor is delayed. The delivery", "    time check denies page two immediately; later processing records the transition and starts derivative cleanup.", "16. Dataset N changes from broad to restricted during a paged query. Its authorization epoch changes, so the next", "    page fails through the restart/refusal path without updating every client's dataset-access revision.", "17. The audience-policy or grant authority is unavailable or inconsistent. Restricted platform record and catalog", "    reads fail closed with the same non-distinguishing error used for unavailable data.", "18. An operator disables D during a paged query. The next page fails through restart/refusal. Re-enabling D under", "    unchanged audience policy uses a fresh reader attestation referencing the current policy's existing exposure", "    accounting; any audience/policy widening requires attributed human approval and new exposure accounting first.", "19. A client-facing catalog request cannot use raw `ingest_dataset_reader` access to reveal D's metadata.", "20. A destination subscription permits D only for client A. The destination rejects broad mode and any grant for B.", "21. A platform signal is derived from restricted D and broad N. Its audience is no broader than D's audience; if", "    the pipeline cannot preserve that inherited audience, it cannot publish the signal.", "22. Broad event E has a restricted-platform `ENRICHES` attachment from D and a tenant-scoped attachment owned by", "    A. Entitled A sees the attachments it is authorized to read; unentitled C sees the unchanged broad event and no", "    edge, count, handle, citation, snippet, score change, or metadata revealing either attachment.", "23. Revoking A's access to D hides D's attachment and invalidates its continuations without re-versioning or", "    changing the broad event E.", "24. A restricted source would change E's canonical summary or merge identity. The write is rejected as an", "    attachment mutation; the source must remain enrichment or produce a separately restricted canonical object.", "25. A Signal computed from E and its restricted attachment is restricted to the attachment's audience. If the", "    engine cannot inherit that audience, it cannot use the attachment as an input.", "26. Commercial news and SEC filings retain distinct source scopes and datasets. The existing commercial-news event", "    scope is reclassified in the registry as the shared-commercial-events scope without changing its stable id,", "    event UUIDs, or history. News keeps its implicit same-scope route. The same approved change activates an SEC", "    event-producing output class and its `SOURCED` binding to the existing shared scope, so no SEC implicit route is", "    ever effective. Reports of the same acquisition may establish one platform event while both evidence chains and", "    source policies remain distinct.", "27. Dataset D has one effective `SOURCED` platform target, an `ENRICHES` binding to client A's standing", "    pool, and no binding to client B. The same contribution may establish the platform event and attach to A's", "    local event, but it cannot affect B's pool and cannot change A's canonical fields.", "28. Dataset D is authorized for destination-local `SOURCED` use by client A. The destination imports one", "    platform-owned copy, then independently synthesizes tenant event A-1 with origin lineage. Origin event P-1", "    remains origin-controlled; A-1 has a different UUID and canonical history, and no client-derived change flows", "    back to P-1.", "29. A dataset is entitled to a client and has a standing-pool binding but no operation-pool binding. It may", "    participate in the client's standing pool but cannot silently fan into existing or newly created operations.", "    An attributed operation workflow adds the specific binding before processing begins.", "30. The same origin contribution is delivered twice and is eligible for two local pools. Pool-qualified", "    idempotency produces at most one effect in each pool; neither attempt adopts, merges, or acknowledges the", "    other's ledger result.", "31. An imported origin platform-event reference is replayed through the destination importer. The importer updates", "    the read-only replica idempotently and never routes the replica as a new locally canonical platform event.", "32. A revoked dataset previously supplied one of several contributions to tenant event A-1. The destination", "    retracts that exact pool-qualified contribution and publishes the recomputed event version from the surviving", "    evidence. If no evidence remains, it tombstones A-1; it never leaves revoked text, embeddings, scores, or", "    canonical fields in place merely because the source is no longer retrievable.", "33. Dataset N's event-producing output is explicitly redirected from its source scope to the shared-events platform", "    scope. New contributions affect only the shared-events pool; the implicit source-scope route is disabled, so", "    ESS does not create duplicate platform truth in both scopes.", "34. Dataset D's retention or license treatment is stricter than the proposed target platform pool. Activation", "    rejects the `SOURCED` binding unless the pool adopts a treatment no broader than D's; it cannot rely on later", "    Retrieval filtering to repair an over-broad canonical event.", "35. A tenant event receives both first-party evidence and an authorized platform contribution. The event remains", "    tenant-owned; both contributions preserve distinct provenance and lineage, and no tenant contribution or", "    derived field can enter the platform event pool.", "36. The shared-events platform scope publishes events under its registered derived platform dataset. Retrieval", "    admits an event only when that derived dataset's audience, eligibility cutoff, security, and lifecycle policy", "    admit the viewer; it never substitutes the news or SEC contributor's dataset as the event's policy owner.", "37. A source dataset narrows after contributing to a shared event. Activation suspends the now-incompatible", "    binding and retracts/recomputes the source's contributions before the broader derived event dataset may continue", "    serving; alternatively the same approved change narrows the derived dataset policy.", "38. A destination imports a platform replica but activates no tenant-pool binding. Retrieval may expose the", "    authorized read-only origin reference, but the portable contribution produces no destination-local canonical", "    event.", "39. Dataset D has `general_retrieval` but lacks `signal_pattern_judgment_eligible`. A tenant event publication", "    dataset that permits Signal/Pattern judgment cannot admit D as `SOURCED`; routing D through a tenant pool never", "    launders it into a consumer for which the source is ineligible.", "40. A source dataset using its implicit default route narrows after contributing events. The effective route and", "    live lineage are re-evaluated even though no binding row exists; the event dataset narrows or the source's", "    contributions retract/recompute before broader serving continues.", "41. A dataset retargets, leaving attributed event history in its old pool, and later narrows. Live lineage causes", "    both old and new pools to re-evaluate and clean up as required; retiring the old binding does not orphan its", "    derived influence outside policy enforcement.", "42. A clean existing platform-news event keeps the same UUID and `platform_scope_id` after the scope registry is", "    reclassified as shared commercial events. A later SEC contribution enters through its explicit `SOURCED`", "    binding and may merge into that event under the normal policy and identity rules; the existing event receives", "    only additive policy pointers, not a new identity, duplicate pool, or migration rewrite.", "43. The news supplier agreement ends while SEC contributions remain valid in the shared pool. The news dataset and", "    its contributions are destroyed or retracted at source/dataset grain, and affected events recompute from their", "    remaining contributions; the shared pool's scope-grain destruction epoch does not advance.", "44. A tenant pool has no tenant event publication dataset. Activation of a non-default platform `SOURCED` binding", "    fails closed until the dataset is registered and every current event row in that pool carries its id.", "45. A new news dataset with licensing narrower than the shared event publication dataset is added to the reused", "    commercial-news scope. Its event-producing output-class activation is rejected even though it would use the", "    implicit default route; provisioning-time checks on older datasets do not grandfather it.", "46. A legacy event in the reused scope contains one contribution from a proven platform-news dataset and one from", "    a client manual upload or fan-out copy. Before the derived event dataset serves it or SEC activates, the client-", "    origin contribution is retracted and the event recomputes from the news contribution. If no proven platform", "    contribution remains, the event is tombstoned; the client evidence is never relabeled as platform data.", "47. A client-tagged manual upload arrives after `DAT-11030`'s attributable live-deployment cursor. It cannot create", "    or change an event in the shared-commercial-events pool and routes to its exact authorized tenant", "    standing/operation pool; an attempted shared-pool commit fails atomically with a bounded typed reason, no", "    Accelerate quarantine row, and no canonical effect. A platform-tagged upload may enter the shared pool only when its source", "    item is lineage-stamped to an authorized registered platform dataset. An account fan-out copy preserves the", "    original source ownership, scope, and dataset and never widens them from the destination account.", "48. Tenant pool T already contains first-party events from dataset F, which lacks", "    `signal_pattern_judgment_eligible`. Registering a tenant event publication dataset that carries that tag fails", "    because F's effective default route and live lineage are revalidated; binding an eligible platform dataset D", "    cannot launder F-only events into Signal/Pattern judgment.", "49. An operator attempts to bind a tenant-owned standing dataset as `SOURCED` into an operation pool or platform", "    pool. Activation fails because V1 non-default bindings accept only PLATFORM-owned source datasets; the governed", "    tenant cross-scope result remains an association, not a canonical contribution.", "", "## 12. Delivery boundary", "", "This capability spans retrieval authorization and event participation, not byte intake:", "", "1. **Audience and entitlement contract:** audience modes, entitlement authority, grant history, epoch behavior,", "   operator workflow, and threat model.", "2. **Store and Retrieval enforcement:** effective-scope binding, authoritative row floor, metadata projection,", "   pre-candidate filtering, delivery recheck, audit, and negative test matrix.", "3. **Derived-work behavior:** cross-scope relationship hydration and post-revocation handling.", "4. **Event-pool contract:** typed-scope pool resolution, dataset-output participation bindings,", "   pool-qualified idempotency, role enforcement, and negative cross-pool tests. The default same-scope mapping", "   preserves the current shared-spine delivery; non-default routing is later work.", "5. **Cross-deployment seam only:** identity, subscription, authorized dependency closure, origin-reference", "   handling, destination-local synthesis, loop prevention, and revocation contract consumed later by `DAT-9083`.", "", "### 12.1 Existing platform-dataset rollout", "", "Audience enforcement must not be enabled by applying a permissive fallback to existing data. Rollout is:", "", "1. add the versioned audience-policy and durable grant-authority support;", "2. inventory every active platform dataset;", "3. record an attributed, human-approved policy with exposure accounting for each one \u2014 commercial news and SEC", "   filings are intended `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS`, while other corpora are decided individually;", "4. create each required explicit grant only after its own attributed approval and exposure accounting, then verify", "   it;", "5. prove no active platform dataset lacks a current audience policy; and", "6. only then make audience policy mandatory and activate the Retrieval branch.", "", "Before a new platform event-producing output goes live, onboarding also provisions or identifies the target", "platform event scope and its derived event dataset, validates every `SOURCED` input against that policy, and", "freezes the one effective platform target. For the initial commercial rollout, the target is the existing", "commercial-news event scope: retain its stable id and event history, reclassify its registry meaning as shared", "commercial events, and register a new derived event dataset\u2014distinct from the commercial-news source dataset\u2014as", "the pool's sole serving-policy owner. For history proven platform-owned, existing event UUIDs, identities, merge", "history, and content are not rewritten; clean platform-news rows receive only the additive `platform_scope_id` and", "derived `dataset_id` policy-pointer stamp, subject to the legacy-origin cleanup gate below. News keeps its existing", "implicit route. SEC keeps its", "separate source scope. Until SEC's explicit `SOURCED` binding to", "the shared-commercial-events scope is active, SEC declares no active event-producing output class: the SEC scope is", "source-only, with no event pool, derived event dataset, or implicit event route. Declaring that output class and", "activating its binding are one approved change. Filings ingested before then produce no events unless a separately", "approved bounded backfill runs. Rollout creates no second pool, dual write, event-identity migration, or wholesale", "re-synthesis. Any later retarget remains forward-only unless an explicit bounded migration retracts and", "re-synthesizes the affected history.", "", "Before SEC's `SOURCED` binding activates, every existing contribution link in the reused scope is attributed to its", "exact news source dataset. The rollout may backfill explicit lineage or use a registered deterministic rule proving", "that a legacy document-path contribution belongs to exactly one commercial-news dataset. If more than one news", "dataset already feeds the scope, every legacy document must be mapped to its exact dataset; a scope-wide inferred", "default is not sufficient. `DAT-11082` owns the bounded contribution-lineage classification/stamp. `DAT-11075`", "separately owns only the null-to-exact event-level derived-dataset pointer backfill and cutover proof; it never", "guesses contribution lineage.", "", "That lineage pass classifies every legacy contribution in the reused scope. A contribution proven to originate in", "a registered commercial-news platform dataset is stamped with that exact dataset. A contribution traced to a", "client upload, account fan-out copy, or any other non-platform origin\u2014and any contribution whose origin cannot be", "proven\u2014is never attributed to news. Before the derived event dataset serves under this model and before SEC's", "`SOURCED` binding activates, those contributions are removed through the ordinary pool-qualified", "retract/recompute/tombstone path. `DAT-11082` owns this bounded classification and cleanup through the", "pool-qualified primitive in `DAT-11076`.", "This may create a cleaned successor version or tombstone for an affected event, but it is not a wholesale identity", "migration or re-synthesis of proven platform-news history.", "", "From `DAT-11030`'s attributable live-deployment cursor onward, every live contribution carries exact stamped source", "dataset/version, route-validation, target-pool, and derived-event-dataset lineage. The registered deterministic rule", "is historical-classification-only for `DAT-11082`; it is not an alternative to live stamping. Once `DAT-11075`", "enables `UNI-22067`'s gate, a missing or mismatched stamp fails atomically with a bounded typed reason and no", "quarantine row or canonical effect. An explicitly platform-tagged manual upload may enter only through an", "authorized registered platform dataset. A client-tagged manual upload routes to its exact tenant standing/operation", "pool; an account fan-out copy preserves the original source ownership, scope, and dataset rather than inheriting", "visibility from the destination account. The derived shared event dataset does not begin serving until tenant-scoped", "ESS can preserve that client path. This is a standing admission invariant, not a recurring cleanup job.", "", "`UNI-22067` owns the shared-pool database admission check. `DAT-11030` owns live dataset stamping,", "manual-upload/fan-out routing, paused-write parking, and their negative tests; S11b's tenant-scoped ESS behavior is", "a prerequisite. Both complete before the derived event dataset serves. This live-path work is not added to the", "earlier control/ledger database-foundation packages.", "", "Because the reused scope is both the news source scope and the shared-events pool, news-only retirement, retention", "expiry, supplier destruction, or license termination operates at news dataset/source grain and invokes Section 8's", "pool-qualified retract/recompute/tombstone path. A scope-grain destruction epoch on this scope means destruction of", "the shared-events pool itself and requires separate approval covering every contributor; a news-only action must", "never quiesce or delete SEC-only contributions merely because they share the target event scope.", "", "This design uses the bounded implementation package in Section 12.2 rather than one omnibus rollout ticket.", "Neither the earlier shared-spine database foundation nor `DAT-11100` absorbs audience policy, live routing,", "reconciliation, projection, or serving activation.", "", "Retrieval also provides an attributed, operator-controlled fail-closed disable for the entire platform branch and", "for an individual `dataset_id`. Disabling advances the applicable dataset authorization epoch and immediately", "invalidates bound reads. Operational re-enable under an unchanged audience policy is not widening; its fresh reader", "attestation references that policy's existing exposure accounting. Any audience or policy widening completes the", "normal approval and exposure-accounting path before re-enable.", "", "Platform-owned ingestion, source-specific adapters, bulk transport, scanning, normalization, and canonical", "publication continue under their existing designs and tickets. They need no new transport behavior for this", "capability; they only need to preserve `platform_scope_id`, `dataset_id`, declared output class, immutable", "contribution identity, and the ordinary provenance needed by Retrieval and later event routing.", "", "### 12.2 Shared-commercial pool state, evidence, and projection contract", "", "The initial shared-commercial dataset has audience mode `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS`. Its current policy and", "authorization epoch are immutable-fact driven. A policy meaning change emits `POLICY_VERSION_CHANGED` and", "revalidates only affected routes. Suspending a route that affects an ACTIVE pool also moves that pool to", "`SERVING_DISABLED` and advances its derived-dataset authorization epoch in the same transaction; compatible-route", "ingestion continues. An epoch-only reader invalidation emits `AUTHORIZATION_EPOCH_ADVANCED` and does not suspend", "otherwise compatible ingestion routes. Existing catalog-activator authority publishes reviewed", "operator facts; runtime readers and writers cannot mint policy, approval, evidence, or state transitions.", "This rollout does not create per-client grant rows. `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS`, the grant authority, and", "client `dataset_access_revision` changes remain fail-closed until their separately approved package ships.", "", "Publication state and emergency write pause are orthogonal:", "", "- `STAGED` is incomplete or not yet proven.", "- `READY` is reconciled, complete, and still unserved.", "- `ACTIVE` is the only servable state.", "- `SERVING_DISABLED` invalidates serving while valid ingestion continues.", "- emergency pause blocks additive canonical/link writes and REINSTATE but permits RETRACT,", "  recompute-to-remove, destruction, and lifecycle cleanup.", "", "Platform Retrieval reads current serving posture through one checked, reader-safe function owned by", "`UNI-22069`. It returns only the current pool state and the revisions needed for bind/invalidation. The matching", "`retrieval_service_<env>` login may execute it but receives no raw operational-table access and no state-transition", "authority.", "", "The canonical-writer guard ships disabled with the schema. The admission gate and writer guard are irreversible and", "are enabled together only after `DAT-11075` inventories every production path in both repos that inserts", "`source_event_changes` or updates `last_change_id`. Its cutover receipt proves `DAT-11030`'s create,", "legacy-document merge, and scoring/backfill writers; `DAT-10452`'s source-item writer; `DAT-11076`'s lifecycle and", "stale-sweep/invalidate writers; and any live Accelerate-side writer are adapted, all older writer/retry sources are", "drained, and the null-to-exact event-pointer backfill is complete. Gate enforcement requires exact event,", "pool, derived-dataset, source-dataset/version, and route-validation lineage on both link families. The only allowed", "original-link update is the checked one-time fill of previously-null rollout lineage; identity/content and non-null", "lineage remain immutable. RETRACT/REINSTATE state lives in append-only per-link sequence history, and REINSTATE", "consumes one exact unexpired approval atomically. Every lifecycle-work producer locks the affected pool row before", "append, after any policy/route locks in the shared order. Claims use a five-minute lease token and generation;", "complete/release requires both, reclaim consumes an attempt, and a stale worker is a no-op. Retryable outcomes are", "serialization/deadlock, transient DataLake/Accelerate/provider unavailability, bounded timeout/deadline, and", "`RETRYABLE_STALE_BASIS`; every other outcome fails immediately. Lifecycle work gets four total attempts per", "re-drive generation (initial, then 1s/5s/15s), and retry returns it to pending with `due_at`. An expired attempt-four", "lease becomes `failed` as `lease_expired_exhausted` without a fifth claim. Exhaustion moves the row to failed. A failed lifecycle-work row is incomplete for READY and remains", "alerting until the existing catalog-activator capability performs an activation-id/digest-idempotent re-drive", "through the reviewed-manifest workflow. That starts a new bounded generation while retaining prior attempt/result", "evidence; runtime ESS can only claim/complete/release and cannot re-drive. Per-item operation ids include lifecycle", "work id, generation, link family, and link id; re-drive skips applied items and reruns every other item under the new", "generation's id. Lifecycle work has no quarantine state.", "", "After enforcement, an existing-event writer locks and CASes the event row before allocating its change id. A", "new-event writer instead inserts the invisible-in-transaction event with null `last_change_id`, appends `created`,", "and CASes the pointer from null before commit. Both forms commit current state, exactly one change row, and the", "pointer atomically.", "", "`STAGED -> READY` locks the pool, then uses bounded SHARE locks and indexed per-pool existence checks to wait out", "pre-gate writers and prove zero active missing/mismatched pointer, route, or contribution lineage and no pending,", "leased, or failed Accelerate lifecycle work. Lifecycle-work creation also requires the pool lock, so no work can", "appear between this check and READY commit. DataLake parked/requeue-row completeness comes from the validated", "`DAT-11082` reconciliation receipt; the Accelerate function does not read DataLake. Lock failure is", "`event_pool_ready_lock_timeout`; the operator transaction also has a bounded statement timeout. READY records the", "typed cutover and reconciliation evidence by id/digest/revision. It does not activate Retrieval.", "", "The current shared-event projection is driven by a transactionally captured pending-work table, not a global", "change-id watermark. Each committed relevant event change creates one work row in the same transaction. Workers", "claim with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, apply idempotently by event/change, and maintain per-event ordering; cross-event", "serials carry no ordering promise. Receipt creation uses one repeatable-read snapshot and requires no pending,", "leased, or unquarantined failed projection work visible to it. Its snapshot time is evidence, never a cursor.", "`UNI-22081` grants its projection tables and checked claim/complete/release functions directly to the matching", "`retrieval_service_<env>` login. That login receives write access only to those projection-owned surfaces; it gains", "no audience-policy mutation, evidence-publication, quarantine-release, or pool-state transition authority.", "", "Projection health fails closed for overdue due/claimed work or an unquarantined terminal failure. A valid", "event-specific quarantine makes that event `EXCLUDED_FAILED` and removes stale content from serving while other", "events recover. The quarantine row and failed work row never mutate. A later correction is restored only through", "append-only `event_pool_projection_quarantine_releases`, after the newer change projects successfully and current", "revisions match.", "", "`READY -> ACTIVE` requires the historical reconciliation/READY receipt plus a current reader attestation, healthy", "projection receipt, policy version, authorization epoch, pool revision, route revisions, and proof that every", "route-suspension lifecycle-work row for the pool is applied. Pending, leased, and failed work all block activation.", "Every policy activation, route suspension, epoch advance, serving", "disable, and ACTIVE transition locks all referenced source/target policy-and-epoch rows sorted by dataset id, route", "rows sorted by route id, then pool rows sorted by pool id before mutation and revision comparison. `ACTIVE ->", "SERVING_DISABLED` advances the epoch", "in the same transaction. Operational re-enable under an unchanged audience policy is not widening: it obtains a", "fresh reader attestation that references the current policy's existing exposure accounting and a fresh projection", "receipt at the current policy and epoch, supplies current pool/route revisions, proves every route-suspension work", "row is applied, and retains the original", "reconciliation receipt only as historical proof. Any audience or policy widening completes the ordinary approval", "and exposure-accounting path first. Every activation id is idempotent with its canonical argument digest;", "conflicting reuse fails", "`idempotency_key_reused`.", "", "Implementation ownership is split so each review stays bounded: `UNI-22066` audience; `UNI-22049` shared schema;", "`UNI-22067` admission; `UNI-22069` evidence/state/pause; `DAT-11030` live routing/parking; `DAT-11075` cutover;", "`DAT-11076` subtractive recompute; `DAT-11078` lifecycle/requeue consumption; `DAT-11082` reconciliation/READY;", "`UNI-22081` projection; `UNI-22082` Retrieval activation. `DAT-11034` is the later local non-default binding;", "`DAT-9083` remains the later cross-account distribution checkpoint.", "", "## 13. Resolved design decisions and per-dataset inputs", "", "The architectural forks are resolved:", "", "1. audience modes are `ALL_ELIGIBLE_CLIENTS` and `EXPLICIT_CLIENT_GRANTS`;", "2. Accelerate owns durable restricted-platform grant authority behind Platform Retrieval;", "3. grants are dataset-level; when individual records require different client audiences, use separate datasets", "   rather than record-level grant lists;", "4. post-revocation policy may retain genuine client-authored judgment when the supplier agreement permits, but", "   always removes source material and anything that reconstructs or exposes it;", "5. V1 uses the same Retrieval verb with separate server-constrained tenant/operation and shared-dataset calls;", "6. dataset identity, data scope, audience, and event pool are separate axes;", "7. event participation is a versioned deployment-local binding per dataset output class and target pool/selector,", "   with role `SOURCED` or `ENRICHES`; scope-default `SOURCED` participation is implicit, while absent non-default", "   authority fails closed; and", "8. a destination may synthesize distinct local tenant events from authorized platform contributions but never", "   becomes a writer of the origin platform event; and", "9. the initial commercial shared-events pool reuses the existing commercial-news event scope and history; its", "   registry meaning broadens to shared commercial events, while SEC retains a separate source scope and reaches the", "   pool only through an explicit `SOURCED` binding.", "", "Each dataset still requires ordinary governed input: audience mode, supplier/agreement reference, security floor,", "eligibility policy, maximum derivative treatment, responsible approver, any initial explicit grants, declared", "event-producing output classes, and each allowed event-pool role/selector. Each platform event scope additionally", "requires its derived event dataset and policy. A tenant pool that receives a non-default platform `SOURCED` route", "additionally requires its one tenant-owned event publication dataset and policy before activation. Those are", "configuration and approval inputs, not unresolved architecture.", "", "Before implementation begins, Section 12 must be mapped to existing or new Jira work with explicit dependencies:", "audience-policy and grant authority first; store/Retrieval and catalog enforcement next; derivative cleanup and the", "rollout gate after those foundations. The default pool resolution stays compatible with the shared-spine epic.", "Non-default event participation and `DAT-9083` cross-account distribution remain separately scheduled later", "capabilities and must not be smuggled into the current database-foundation tickets. In particular, SEC source", "ingestion may land before that routing capability, but SEC event publication into the shared-commercial-events pool", "must wait for the explicit binding and its enforcement path.", "", "## 14. Parent-document amendments after approval", "", "This remains the single detailed specification. Approval should produce only narrow references elsewhere:", "", "- `DESIGN-data-ingestion.md`: state that a platform-owned publication preserves platform scope, dataset identity,", "  declared output class, and contribution identity; distinguish the source record's scope from the deployment-local", "  event pool; make the existing ESS scope-equality/FK rule target-pool-local while preserving the contributing", "  source item's separately validated scope and both destruction epochs; and govern client distribution here rather", "  than through intake provenance.", "- `SPEC-shared-spine.md`: state that the platform scope and dataset seams support both broad and restricted", "  platform datasets; its scope-default ESS behavior is the default event-pool mapping, while non-default", "  participation, client entitlement, and cross-deployment replication remain outside the spine.", "- Accelerate dataset/catalog design: add the versioned audience-policy record, durable restricted-platform grant", "  authority, per-client dataset-access revision, dataset authorization epoch, catalog RLS/security-barrier", "  enforcement, expiry behavior, lineage requirement, and rollout/disable guard.", "- Platform Retrieval PRD: replace the blanket launch rule that platform rows require a platform principal with the", "  trusted, server-bound platform-dataset audience branch defined here; bind revisions and prohibit projected-store", "  post-filtering.", "- Accelerate event-attachment contract: extend the research-packet/client-artifact seam to exact-version restricted", "  platform and tenant targets, enforce authorization to both endpoints, and prohibit `ENRICHES` attachments from", "  mutating or influencing broadly visible parent-event state.", "- ESS/event design: add the typed target-scope resolver, versioned dataset-output bindings, pool-qualified", "  idempotency and lineage, role enforcement, and destination-local synthesis boundary without weakening the", "  no-cross-owner-canonical-merge rule.", "- Client-data onboarding runbook: collect the explicit audience policy, widening approval, derivative treatment,", "  exposure accounting, and initial grant inputs without committing real customer configuration to source control.", "- `DAT-9083`: consume the dataset audience, global identity, destination-local grant, portable contribution,", "  origin-reference, event-pool binding, loop-prevention, and revocation seams without turning local serials or", "  deployment-local pool ids into distributed identity.", "", "No second copy of this design belongs in either parent specification."], "blocks": [{"start": 1, "end": 10, "label": "Design \u2014 Restricted Distribution and Event Participation of Platform-Owned Datasets"}, {"start": 11, "end": 35, "label": "1. Purpose"}, {"start": 36, "end": 82, "label": "2. Decision summary"}, {"start": 83, "end": 293, "label": "3. Ownership, dataset, and audience are different axes"}, {"start": 294, "end": 347, "label": "4. Dataset audience policy"}, {"start": 348, "end": 413, "label": "5. Client-to-dataset entitlement"}, {"start": 414, "end": 511, "label": "6. Retrieval enforcement"}, {"start": 512, "end": 605, "label": "7. Client-derived work and cross-scope links"}, {"start": 606, "end": 651, "label": "8. Revocation and contract change"}, {"start": 652, "end": 714, "label": "9. Cross-deployment distribution seam"}, {"start": 715, "end": 745, "label": "10. Security invariants"}, {"start": 746, "end": 883, "label": "11. Acceptance scenarios"}, {"start": 884, "end": 1080, "label": "12. Delivery boundary"}, {"start": 1081, "end": 1116, "label": "13. Resolved design decisions and per-dataset inputs"}, {"start": 1117, "end": 1147, "label": "14. Parent-document amendments after approval"}], "lineCount": 1147, "sha256": "bbc5eb4866df492c7399fd46f0c15167b477fd3ff47d11157956a0da1e7efff7", "endsWithNewline": true});
